<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        A comment placed ABOVE <CustomField> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with the
        misleading error "unable to find matching parent xml file for CustomField".

        'Buyer' ADDED 2026-08-09 (Tranche 3C). Token contract, identical to
        LOI__c.Ball_In_Court__c and to both Direction__c fields:

          Us     = DPEG. Correct on BOTH sides - side-neutral.
          Seller = THE COUNTERPARTY on an ACQUISITION, and the incumbent wire token that
                   PsaVersionService already writes. It is NOT removed.
          Buyer  = THE COUNTERPARTY on a DISPOSITION.

        WHY THIS FILE IS IN 3C AT ALL. It was not on the dispatched file list, but the
        Disposition_PSA record type cannot expose a correct counterparty token unless one
        exists in this master value set: a record type may only restrict the values a field
        already has, and this field is <restricted>true</restricted>, so Apex cannot write a
        token that is absent here either. Shipping the record type without this value would
        leave a sell-side PSA whose only counterparty option is 'Seller' - which on a sale
        names DPEG, the same party 'Us' already names. That is the exact inversion the record
        type exists to fix, so omitting it would have made 3C self-defeating rather than merely
        incomplete. Recorded here because it is a scope addition, not an inherited decision.

        ADD ONLY - NOTHING IS RENAMED. A rename is a removal plus an add: it triggers the
        standing picklist-removal sweep rule and forces a migration on live acquisition rows
        that are correctly 'Seller' today, for zero benefit.

        UNLIKE Counter_Offer__c AND PSA_Version__c, THIS FIELD'S OBJECT DOES HAVE RECORD TYPES,
        so the restriction here is real rather than service-enforced: Acquisition_PSA exposes
        Us + Seller only, Disposition_PSA exposes Us + Buyer only. ⚠ Record-type picklist
        restriction is UI-ONLY and is NOT enforced by Apex DML, so it stops a user picking
        'Seller' on a sale but does not stop code writing it. PsaVersionService is the only
        writer and is where that is actually enforced.
    -->
    <fullName>Ball_In_Court__c</fullName>
    <description>Whose turn it is in the PSA negotiation. Flipped automatically by PsaVersionController when a version is logged (their version -&gt; Us; our version -&gt; the counterparty). The counterparty token differs by record type: Seller on an acquisition PSA, Buyer on a disposition PSA, because on a sale DPEG is itself the seller.</description>
    <label>Ball In Court</label>
    <required>false</required>
    <trackHistory>false</trackHistory>
    <trackTrending>false</trackTrending>
    <type>Picklist</type>
    <valueSet>
        <restricted>true</restricted>
        <valueSetDefinition>
            <sorted>false</sorted>
            <value>
                <fullName>Us</fullName>
                <default>false</default>
                <label>Us</label>
            </value>
            <value>
                <fullName>Seller</fullName>
                <default>false</default>
                <label>Seller</label>
            </value>
            <value>
                <fullName>Buyer</fullName>
                <default>false</default>
                <label>Buyer</label>
            </value>
        </valueSetDefinition>
    </valueSet>
</CustomField>
